title: "Experiment readout: proactive activity suggestions"
subtitle: "Does a nudge get people moving in the next 30 minutes? (HeartSteps V1)"
---



::: {.callout-note}
## Status: results added

The analysis plan at the bottom of this page was committed before any outcome was compared (commit e39c0a7). The results were computed and committed afterwards (b3e15ea), exactly as planned. The plan text below is unchanged.
:::



::: {.callout-tip}
## Result: Go, under the pre-registered rule

A suggestion increased steps in the next 30 minutes by **\+16\.1% \(95% CI \+0\.5% to \+34\.2%\)**, about \+38 steps on a 235-step average, with no guardrail flags. The effect is real but fragile and short-lived: the confidence interval only just excludes zero, and the effect fades to nothing within about a month.
:::

## Results

The analysis below follows the plan further down this page exactly. The plan was committed first (e39c0a7) and the results second (b3e15ea); there are no deviations.

### The headline, and how confident to be

- **Primary:** \+16\.1% \(95% CI \+0\.5% to \+34\.2%\), p = 0\.043. The published analysis of the same trial found +14% (p = .06), so the two agree closely.
- **Sensitivity:** treating missing step counts as zero gives \+12\.2% \(95% CI \-1\.3% to \+27\.7%\), p = 0\.078. The same evidence lands on either side of p = .05 depending on how missing data is handled, so the effect should be read as modest and uncertain, not as proven.

